**Definition 1.2 (the candidate finite boundary).** The observer receives
one joint handoff after an unknown original past and before the next public
request. All information that can assist a subsequent live operation must
factor through one fixed finite classical–quantum algebra. Earlier acquired
archives may have arbitrary joint dependence on height, phase and the entire
signed winding. No later source-bearing input or interaction is available
outside that algebra. Source-independent computation, blanks, randomness,
clocks and exact continuous output recording are allowed. Section 4 states
this access condition precisely, including every acquisition outcome.

The following are distinct tasks and conclusions:

| Task under that access condition | Result |
| --- | --- |
| One fixed positive horizontal request, exact on an interval of heights | Its failure CDFs have unbounded real linear span; no fixed finite handoff realizes them all exactly. |
| The same request on $M$ heights and their full nuisance orbits | The ideal reduced output span has dimension exactly $M+5$; this gives a necessary operator-space dimension bound. |
| The same request with nonzero complete-output error | Finitely many failure events give an explicit positive error floor for every fixed finite algebra. |
| Phase requests with a fixed finite action bound | A finite-action packing inequality is necessary. The unrestricted-action constant is not asserted for this bounded menu. |
| All phase requests of individually finite action | Uniform complete-output error is at least $\sqrt{Z(1-Z)}$, equal to $1/2$ at the balanced source. |
| The phase-only task in a relaxed common-CPTP comparison class | Retaining the specimen and applying a common pinching attains that constant. No original-menu implementation is supplied. |

These are necessary subconsumers of the full observer. None supplies
unknown-past acquisition, a total unknown-height guard, all-prefix feedback,
or an upper bound for the original full future menu.

